{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785260962,"nonce":"Re++DrKXIRKChMvU5fB0ed1YQ8I=","title":"ADR-0065 s1: implement Cache — an explicit, author-declared, keyed DIRECTORY cache","message":"ADR-0007 has named **Cache** as the better-fitting concept for `~/.cargo` / `node_modules` since the beginning, and it **does not exist**: no migration, no `cache_key`, nothing in `scarab-engine` or `scarab-pipeline`. So today the only place a dependency tree or a build output can live is the Workspace, which is why retaining workspaces is expensive.\n\nThis implements ADR-0007 **as originally written** and reverses nothing: the 2026-07-26 amendment forbids making Cache *mandatory for speed*, not making it available.\n\n**A keyed directory cache, never a shared mutable mount.** BuildKit-style `--mount=type=cache` was rejected on **concurrency**, not cost: two concurrent Runs writing one `~/.cargo/registry` or `node_modules` is contention at best and corruption at worst — BuildKit serialises cache mounts for exactly this reason, and serialising CI steps on a shared cache is an availability problem that grows with usage. The usual argument for a mount (copying a 1 GB tree per Step is brutal) is answered by storing a Cache as a content-addressed tree: it rides ADR-0062's Farm/Export machinery and materialises **lazily**, so a Step touching 5% of `node_modules` moves 5%.\n\n- [ ] Author declares directories + a key (typically a lockfile hash); restore at Step start, save at Step end\n- [ ] Stored as a content-addressed tree, reusing the Farm/Export path — not a second storage mechanism\n- [ ] Best-effort and evictable; **never evidence**, which is what puts it outside ADR-0064's durability rules\n- [ ] A miss is slower and never wrong — assert it, because that property is the whole licence for evicting a Cache\n- [ ] No automatic detection (see the ticket closing 165c2dc): the author asks or nothing happens","files":null}]}